      {
        "id": "s4",
        "kind": "worked-example",
        "title": "Equation from a gradient and a point",
        "body": "Put the gradient in, substitute the point to find $c$, then write the whole equation. The second example has a negative gradient and a negative coordinate, which is where signs go wrong; keep the brackets in until the numbers are settled.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Gradient 3, through $(2, 7)$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "$y = 3x + c$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$7 = 3(2) + c = 6 + c$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$c = 1$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = 3x + 1$"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Gradient $-\\tfrac{1}{2}$, through $(-4, 3)$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "$y = -\\tfrac{1}{2}x + c$"
              },
              {
                "text": "Substitute $(-4, 3)$: $3 = -\\tfrac{1}{2}(-4) + c$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$3 = 2 + c$, so $c = 1$",
                "note": "Minus a half times minus four is plus two"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = -\\tfrac{1}{2}x + 1$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s5",
        "kind": "worked-example",
        "title": "Equation from two points",
        "body": "Gradient first, then the same method as before with either point. When the gradient comes out as a fraction, keep it as a fraction; a decimal like $0.33$ is not exact and loses the accuracy mark.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Through $(-1, 5)$ and $(2, -4)$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "$m = \\dfrac{-4 - 5}{2 - (-1)} = \\dfrac{-9}{3} = -3$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = -3x + c$, using $(2, -4)$: $-4 = -6 + c$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$c = 2$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = -3x + 2$"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Through $(3, 1)$ and $(9, 5)$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "$m = \\dfrac{5 - 1}{9 - 3} = \\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{2}{3}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = \\tfrac{2}{3}x + c$, and use $(3, 1)$: $1 = \\tfrac{2}{3} \\times 3 + c = 2 + c$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$c = -1$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = \\tfrac{2}{3}x - 1$",
                "note": "Check with the other point: $\\tfrac{2}{3} \\times 9 - 1 = 5$ ✓"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
