      {
        "id": "s5b",
        "kind": "explain",
        "title": "Midpoint and length",
        "body": "The **midpoint** of $A(x_1, y_1)$ and $B(x_2, y_2)$ is the average of each coordinate:\n\n$$\\left(\\dfrac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\dfrac{y_1 + y_2}{2}\\right)$$\n\nThe **length** of $AB$ comes from Pythagoras on the change in $x$ and the change in $y$:\n\n$$AB = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}$$\n\nFor $A(1, 3)$ and $B(9, 9)$: midpoint $(5, 6)$, and $AB = \\sqrt{8^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{100} = 10$.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "diagram",
            "component": "line-graph",
            "props": {
              "xRange": [
